00:56:03,661 --> 00:56:04,660
That's where it runs the wheels as electric motors.

868
00:56:04,661 --> 00:56:10,160
as electric motors, and instead of just a battery that you plug into the wall, it has a fuel

869
00:56:10,161 --> 00:56:14,660
cell that converse hydrogen and oxygen into electricity to keep the batteries charged.

870
00:56:14,661 --> 00:56:21,160
In the most common fuel cell type called a PEM fuel cell, short for proton exchange membrane,

871
00:56:21,161 --> 00:56:25,160
hydrogen atoms are stripped of their electrons or ionized.

872
00:56:25,161 --> 00:56:29,160
The positively charged protons pass through the exchange membrane,

873
00:56:29,161 --> 00:56:32,660
while the electrons pass through a circuit to provide electric power.

874
00:56:32,661 --> 00:56:39,200
On the other side, the ions and protons rejoin with oxygen from the air to make H2O or water.

875
00:56:39,920 --> 00:56:44,700
The water vapor is the only tailpipe emission, and the performance is very respectable.

876
00:56:45,520 --> 00:56:49,360
In Ford's car, the fuel cell delivers 85 kilowatts of power.

877
00:56:49,940 --> 00:56:52,120
That's equal to 117 horsepower.

878
00:56:52,520 --> 00:56:54,140
Fuel cells have been around for a long time.

879
00:56:54,260 --> 00:56:57,840
What's new is our ability to put it in an automobile and drive down the road.

880
00:56:57,841 --> 00:57:03,780
History credits English lawyer turned scientist Sir William Robert Grove

881
00:57:03,781 --> 00:57:07,780
as developing what would become known as the fuel cell in 1838.

882
00:57:09,260 --> 00:57:14,220
Grove knew that electricity could be used to split a water molecule into its two elements,

883
00:57:14,480 --> 00:57:17,760
